# Android (BlissOS) analysis-pipeline configuration.
#
# Sourced, never executed. Every consumer resolves its own location first
# and exports REPO_ROOT before sourcing this file:
#
#     SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")" && pwd)"
#     REPO_ROOT="$(cd -- "$SCRIPT_DIR/.." && pwd)"
#     . "$SCRIPT_DIR/platform.env"
#     . "$SCRIPT_DIR/pipeline-android.env"
#
# Nothing here may depend on $PWD, ~, or $HOME.
#
# This is the per-OS half of the C1 file set. The other three Android
# files are run_analysis-android.sh (DRAFT-015), preconfig-android.sh and
# drakvuf-android.sh (DRAFT-016). The clone script is NOT per-OS -- it is
# the shared automation/clone.pl, which reads CLONE_LV_SIZE below from the
# environment.
#
# Every value is ${NAME:-default}, so anything already set in the
# environment wins and CI can override without patching this file.
#
# --- Keep this diffable against pipeline-ubuntu.env ---------------------
#
# The two files should differ only where the two targets genuinely differ.
# An unexplained divergence is a defect, and it is greppable:
#
#     diff <(grep -oE '^[A-Z_]+=' automation/pipeline-ubuntu.env) \
#          <(grep -oE '^[A-Z_]+=' automation/pipeline-android.env)
#
# C4 forbids this file from defining any host-global name. Also greppable:
#
#     grep -nE '^(VG_NAME|ANALYSIS_BRIDGE|UPLINK_BRIDGE|SUBNET_PREFIX)' \
#         automation/pipeline-android.env     # expect: no output
#
# See docs/architecture/analysis-pipeline.md, "Shared and per-OS code
# boundary", for the full C1-C10 contract.

# --- Repository-owned assets (paths under the checkout) -----------------

# The sandbox TEMPLATE domain, not the golden `android`. Clones are made
# from this; the golden VM is never touched by an analysis run.
SANDBOX_DOMAIN="${SANDBOX_DOMAIN:-android_sandbox}"

DOMAIN_CONFIG="${DOMAIN_CONFIG:-$REPO_ROOT/misc/${SANDBOX_DOMAIN}.cfg}"

# LibVMI/DRAKVUF kernel profile. Passed to drakvuf/injector as -r, which
# is the only way it is ever resolved: clone domains are created with
# rewritten names and have no /etc/libvmi.conf stanza of their own.
# Untracked and host-generated -- see profiles/README.md to regenerate.
KERNEL_PROFILE="${KERNEL_PROFILE:-$REPO_ROOT/profiles/android.json}"

# `xl restore` source, produced out-of-band once per sandbox template.
# clone.pl derives the same path independently, from FindBin and its own
# $origin argument, so the two cannot drift apart.
STATE_DIR="${STATE_DIR:-$REPO_ROOT/state}"
SAVED_STATE="${SAVED_STATE:-$STATE_DIR/${SANDBOX_DOMAIN}.bak}"

# Size of the LVM copy-on-write snapshot backing each clone -- the
# exception store, not the clone's disk size.
#
# 32G, against Ubuntu's 64G, and it is measured rather than guessed: the
# android_sandbox LV is 32,00g where ubuntu_sandbox is 64,00g
# (docs/runbooks/verify-android-isolation.md:975), and 32G is what the
# manual clone in that runbook used.
#
# A wrong value here does NOT fail loudly. clone.pl falls back to 64G if
# this never reaches it, and a 64G exception store on a 32G origin works
# fine -- it just reserves twice what it needs from a VG that also holds
# two 64G Ubuntu volumes and a 256G root. Check `lvs` reports 32,00g
# rather than inferring success from a clone that came up.
CLONE_LV_SIZE="${CLONE_LV_SIZE:-32G}"

# --- Runtime data (fixed absolute paths, NOT repo-relative) -------------

# Per-OS subdirectories implement the OS-selection mechanism decided in
# issue #3: the submitter chooses the target OS by choosing where to
# submit, and one dirwatch instance watches each. The OS token is never
# parsed out of a filename or any other attacker-influenced byte.
TARGET_OS="${TARGET_OS:-android}"
WATCH_FOLDER="${WATCH_FOLDER:-/malware_incoming/$TARGET_OS}"
SERVE_FOLDER="${SERVE_FOLDER:-/malware_processing/$TARGET_OS}"
OUTPUT_FOLDER="${OUTPUT_FOLDER:-/malware_finished/$TARGET_OS}"

# --- Guest-side staging -------------------------------------------------

# Where the sample is written inside the analysis guest, as
# <staging dir>/<submitted filename> (C5).
#
# /data/local/tmp, decided 2026-08-10 and execution-evidenced on the guest.
# It satisfies both properties C10 requires and that are NOT visible from
# the path:
#
#   1. it pre-exists in the saved image -- injector's writefile does not
#      create parent directories;
#   2. it permits execution.
#
# The second is why the obvious choice was rejected.
# /storage/self/primary/Download is writable and visible to the injection
# anchor, but sits on a noexec FUSE mount where chmod is silently a no-op,
# so a staged native sample cannot be executed at all. Neither fact shows
# up in `ls` or in an SELinux denial; it took an exec probe.
GUEST_STAGING_DIR="${GUEST_STAGING_DIR:-/data/local/tmp}"

# There is deliberately NO GUEST_USER here. It is Ubuntu's mechanism for
# building a staging path (/home/$GUEST_USER/Desktop) and Android has no
# /home/<user> concept, so an Android copy of it would be a value present
# only because the template had one -- which is the failure C4 exists to
# prevent, in miniature. pipeline-ubuntu.env already says the contract
# term is GUEST_STAGING_DIR, not GUEST_USER.

# --- Analysis-network DNS ----------------------------------------------

# Resolver configured in the clone at analysis time by
# preconfig-android.sh, the way preconfig-ubuntu.sh applies it for Ubuntu.
#
# 1.1.1.1 because Ubuntu's is 1.1.1.1, not on separate reasoning.
# pipeline-ubuntu.env states the rule: this is analysis-network POLICY by
# nature and per-OS only by location, so three pipelines free to set three
# different resolvers is an unreviewable containment posture. Treat
# divergence between the copies as a defect unless it is deliberate and
# written down here.
#
# Nothing is baked into the Android golden image. The template carried an
# in-guest DNAT to 172.16.10.1:53 before the 2026-08-11 rebuild, pointing
# at an address where nothing has ever listened; it was deliberately not
# re-applied (issue #7). Per-clone resolver configuration belongs to
# analysis time, where a per-OS config can override it.
GUEST_NAMESERVER="${GUEST_NAMESERVER:-1.1.1.1}"

# --- APK handling -------------------------------------------------------

# Guest path where preconfig-android.sh leaves the package name it
# resolved, and from which drakvuf-android.sh reads it.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. `pm install` does not report the package it installed
# and Dom0 cannot read it out of the APK (binary AXML, no parser here), so
# the guest identifies it by diffing `pm list packages` around the
# install. That diff used to run in the same injected shell as the launch
# and needed no state at all. Since 2026-08-17 the install is in preconfig
# and the launch is in the trace -- two separate injected shells -- so the
# result has to cross between them.
#
# WHY A GUEST FILE AND NOT A readfile BACK TO DOM0. `injector -m readfile`
# would work; it is evidenced. It would also cost two more injections (the
# read, then a delete) on a pipeline where every injection waits for the
# anchor to be scheduled, and it would read data off the guest, which is
# the shape C7 exists to discourage. This crosses guest-side instead and
# never leaves the VM.
#
# WHY IT IS NOT AN ARTIFACT A SAMPLE CAN FIND. drakvuf-android.sh's launch
# command reads this file and removes it as its first two acts, before
# `am start`. No sample code can run before that point: an Android
# application cannot execute during installation, and stays in the stopped
# state until an explicit launch. The window in which a file naming the
# sample's own package exists is a window in which the sample is not
# running. If the trace never runs, the clone is destroyed with it.
APK_PKG_HANDOFF="${APK_PKG_HANDOFF:-$GUEST_STAGING_DIR/.pm.tmp}"

# Seconds preconfig-android.sh waits after injecting `pm install` before
# returning.
#
# `injector -m execproc` returns when the injected process STARTS, not
# when it finishes, so Dom0 has no completion signal and this sleep is the
# synchronisation. It is not tuning: if preconfig returns early, dirwatch
# starts the trace while the install is still running, and the install
# then finishes under full syscall interception -- which is the >40x
# slowdown this split exists to avoid (verify-android-mvp.md F3).
#
# 20s against a measured 3s for a 12 MB APK (2026-08-17, untraced). The
# ceiling is dirwatch's 60s preconfig timer, which covers this sleep and
# both other injections, and a preconfig timeout is a clone/retry loop.
# Re-measure before raising it, and measure a dexopt-heavy APK before
# trusting the margin.
APK_INSTALL_WAIT="${APK_INSTALL_WAIT:-20}"

# --- Orchestration ------------------------------------------------------

# dirwatch watch mode: 1 = inotify poll, 0 = 1s sleep loop.
WATCH_MODE="${WATCH_MODE:-1}"

# A long-lived process inside the saved memory image that injector
# attaches to. It is a property of the .bak file, not of this config --
# re-saving the template almost certainly changes it.
#
# 3174, paired with saved state sha256 465aa4d7…, from the 2026-08-17
# re-save (docs/checklists/android.md §1.4/§1.5, 2026-08-17 annotations).
# The two are recorded together because neither means anything alone: the
# PID is only valid for the .bak it was captured from.
#
# Superseded values, kept because both still appear in dated evidence and
# a reader will meet them: 3163 (2026-08-11 rebuild, state 1844fc0a…) and
# 3164 (an undated re-save before DRAFT-013, state 516644e2…).
#
# THE ANCHOR MUST BE SCHEDULED, NOT MERELY PRESENT. This is the lesson of
# 2026-08-17 and it cost a whole bring-up run. The anchor was a
# backgrounded `nano` that had taken SIGTTIN on a console tty and was
# saved STOPPED. injector waits for the target PID to be scheduled onto a
# vCPU, has no timeout, and prints nothing while it waits -- so every
# injection hung silently and forever, and dirwatch's uncounted retry
# turned that into an endless clone/hang/kill loop. A vmi-process-list
# match proves the task exists; it does not prove it ever runs.
# See docs/runbooks/verify-android-mvp.md F1.
#
# The current anchor is a backgrounded `ping` against loopback: it is
# scheduled on its own, and because the target is 127.0.0.1 it puts
# nothing on the analysis VLAN, so tcpdump.sh's per-sample pcaps stay free
# of anchor traffic.
#
# Constraints on any replacement, all evidenced (android.md §1.4):
# self-scheduling; root, because preconfig-android.sh imports
# /data/system/environ/classpath which is 0600 system:system and a
# non-root anchor fails SILENTLY; 64-bit, per injector --help; no traffic
# on the analysis VLAN; no console VT.
#
# A stale value also fails SILENTLY: injector attaches to whatever PID it
# is given, with no warning. Re-read it from the live template after any
# re-save, and do NOT build auto-discovery on a process-name grep
# (android.md §1.4).
INJECTION_PID="${INJECTION_PID:-3174}"

# Sizes dirwatch's worker pool AND the number of xenbr1.<n> VLAN
# sub-interfaces network-setup.sh must create. The two must agree.
#
# KNOWN GAP (DRAFT-036): the thread-slot index *is* the VLAN and the slot
# array is process-local, so THIS PIPELINE CANNOT RUN CONCURRENTLY WITH
# UBUNTU'S -- both would allocate VLANs 1..MAX_CLONES and collide on the
# gateway addresses and the isolation rules. Writing this file is safe;
# starting a second dirwatch while the first runs is not, and nothing
# detects it.
#
# CLASS: host-global, deliberately left here to match pipeline-ubuntu.env.
# DRAFT-036 replaces the fixed clone count with memory-based admission
# outright, so moving it to platform.env first would mean moving it twice
# -- and moving it in one copy and not the other is worse than either.
MAX_CLONES="${MAX_CLONES:-2}"